  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "adr-api-versioning-1",
      "type": "adr",
      "label": "ADR: {{VERSIONING_SCHEME}} API versioning",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 180,
      "height": 52,
      "collapsed": false,
      "status": "proposed",
      "date": "",
      "context": "Public and partner APIs must evolve without breaking existing clients, which upgrade on their own schedule. The team needs one rule for what is a breaking change, how a new version is exposed and how long old versions are supported, so that every service does not invent its own policy.",
      "decision": "Version public APIs with {{VERSIONING_SCHEME}}. Additive changes (new optional fields, new endpoints, new enum values where documented as open) are non-breaking and ship within the current version. Removing or renaming fields, changing types or semantics, or tightening validation is breaking and requires a new major version. Deprecated versions remain fully functional for at least {{DEPRECATION_MONTHS}} months, announced via a Deprecation header, the changelog and direct notice to registered consumers.",
      "consequences": "• Clients can rely on a stable contract within a major version and plan migrations.\n• Running two major versions in parallel is normal; the implementation must support it (adapters or parallel routes).\n• The contract-diff check in CI is the enforcement point; a breaking diff fails the build unless the major version changes.\n• Clients must tolerate unknown fields, and this is stated in the API guidelines.\n• Internal service-to-service APIs may use a lighter policy when all consumers are known and deploy together.",
      "alternatives": "1. Header or media-type versioning — cleaner URLs; harder to test in a browser and easy to omit by mistake.\n2. Date-based versioning per account (Stripe style) — fine-grained and gentle for clients; significant implementation complexity.\n3. No versioning, only additive changes forever — simple; accumulates dead fields and eventually forces a big-bang break.\n4. Version per endpoint — maximum flexibility; consumers must track many version numbers."
    }
  ],
  "relations": []
}
